212-/-- The generated `retire` of an activity log (LANG-76; LANG-80: one method for every log, with or
213-without a cursor). Without a cursor (`none`: the offer's session log, or an activity with no
214:patience) it is the one edit, the log's retract at the argument key; the log guard decides
215-(`activityWaiting`: an answered row, or an unanswered one at or past its deadline). With a cursor it
216-reads the log row at its key (the caller's pin, as `resume` does). An answered row leaves the log
217-alone: its cursor row left with the answer. An unanswered row leaves the log AND the cursor in one
218-Turn, which the log guard admits only at or past the row's deadline (`activityLate`, anyone) and the
219-cursor guard admits there too (`activityDequeue`). So the activity's own methods keep exactly one
220-cursor row for each unanswered log row across an abandon: a log-only retraction of an unanswered row
221-would leave a cursor row its resumer can no longer answer. -/
222-def activityRetireMethod (S : Ty) (rels : List (Label × RelDecl)) (log : Label) (ld : RelDecl) :
223-    Option (Label × RelDecl) → Term
224-  | none =>
225-    let f (i : Nat) (l : Label) := Term.get (.var i) l
226-    let key (i : Nat) := activityKeyTerm (f i "agent") (f i "notBefore")
227-    activityMethod S (keyTy ld) (.record []) (activityResult rels (.var 2) [activityEdit rels log ld "retract" (key 1)])
228-  | some (cursor, cd) =>
